user
You are auditing a pool of scoring criteria that were written by other
models. Your job is a ROUTING decision, made blind: you are NOT told who wrote each
criterion or what it was written for.

For each criterion, decide:

  "quality_relevant" -- the criterion measures a property that a careful expert would
      count as genuine evidence about which headlines BBC readers actually click and read most. It is about the substance of
      what is being communicated, the evidence behind it, and the content of the item.

  "incidental" -- the criterion measures something that may well PREDICT how the item
      fares but is not itself which headlines BBC readers actually click and read most: length or verbosity, typography, punctuation
      or capitalisation habits, formatting and trailing furniture, boilerplate or
      template phrasing, community/venue style markers, subject-matter or topic markers,
      temporal tells, orthographic habits, or a textual fingerprint of some upstream
      circumstance of the item's production (who wrote it, with what practice or
      following, when, where in the run of entries).

The corpus is: BBC News headlines: for each captured day, headlines that made the site's most-read module versus other same-day BBC headlines. Each item is a news headline.

SECOND JUDGEMENT, only for criteria you label "incidental". Some incidental channels have
an upstream cause that plausibly ALSO produces genuinely better work (for example, a
practised entrant may both be favoured for irrelevant reasons AND actually write better
entries). Return `mixed`: true when you think the upstream cause plausibly produces real
which headlines BBC readers actually click and read most too, false when the channel looks like pure nuisance. For criteria you label
"quality_relevant", return mixed=false.

Judge each criterion ON ITS OWN TEXT. Do not try to guess which pool it came from, and
do not let the ORDER of the list influence you.

CRITERIA:

[X01] Leading geographic dateline
Mark headlines that begin with “In” followed by a place, or with a country or city name. This can reveal foreign-desk or bureau-origin conventions.

[X02] Practical service promise
High when the headline promises advice, instructions, or help with a decision; low when it offers no usable guidance; it does not apply without a service frame

[X03] Mystery-death hook
Score high when an unexplained death or unexplained cause is the core hook ('something in room', serial killings, radiation spike). Score low when cause of harm is stated.

[X04] Ideological Polarization
Scores high when opposing political, religious, national, or moral camps are identifiable; low when disagreement is technical or no stance is present. It does not apply when group positions are not explicit.

[X05] Location-heavy wording
named geography | DESCRIPTION: Mark headlines containing two or more explicit countries, cities, regions, or national demonyms.

[X06] Celebrity personal-name slot
Headline leads with a bare celebrity name plus colon (Harry:, Matthew Perry:, KSI, Malala), reflecting entertainment desk conventions and promoted sidebar placement.

[X07] Social-Identity Resonance
Scores high when the headline invokes a shared group identity, belonging, or identity-related value; low when no group identity is implicated. It does not apply when relevance requires personal details absent from the headline.

[X08] Withholds exactly the detail the headline makes the reader want
Score 0-10 for whether the headline sets up a specific, legitimate question in the reader's mind whose answer is in the story -- who, how much, what happened next -- without being deceptive about what the story contains. 10 = a precise curiosity gap a reader can only close by reading; 0 = either everything is already in the headline or the gap is vague clickbait that the story cannot pay off.

[X09] Domestic-UK angle tell
Explicit mention of UK, England, or a UK institution (NHS, PM), flagging the home-audience desk whose readership baseline is higher.

[X10] Quoted speech
Mark headlines containing quotation marks around a phrase or sentence. Quoted headlines may fingerprint interview, reaction, or live-news formats.

[X11] Concrete Scene Vividness
Scores high when the headline depicts a specific sensory scene, object, or action that is readily pictured; low when wording is abstract or bureaucratic. It does not apply when no concrete scene is conveyed.

[X12] In-quote ordinary voice
Quoted first-person speech from a non-public figure ("I cut a carrot...", "'litres and litres'") marks magazine/features commissions.

[X13] Numerical specificity
Mark digits, percentages, ages, counts, or precise quantities. This may fingerprint data-led, health, or breaking-news treatments.

[X14] First-person voice
Score high when the headline contains a first-person pronoun or quoted speech ('I', 'we', 'our', 'litres and litres'). Score low when it is entirely third-person institutional reporting.

[X15] Source Transparency
Scores high when the headline identifies a source, evidence, or attribution that clarifies claim provenance; low when it presents an unsupported assertion. It does not apply to plainly observable events without contested claims.

[X16] Follow-up headline ellipticalness
Headline omits full names/events and relies on assumed prior knowledge ("Why Donald Trump lost", "The biggest news wasn't about Manafort"), signalling day 2+ of a running story.

[X17] Running-story recap framing
Mark “what do we know,” “explaining,” “why …,” “latest images,” or similar recap language. Such wording may fingerprint a later position in a multi-day story or a designated explainer slot.

[X18] Explicit question-mark headline
Headline ends in a question mark, e.g. "What do we know?" or "Did...start in...?"

[X19] Explicit emotion word
Headline contains emotion terms (fear, outrage, love, demons, gross). Scores high when emotion is the story; not for neutral reporting

[X20] Signals direct consequence for the reader's own life
Score 0-10 for whether the headline makes clear that the story affects the reader personally -- their money, health, commute, bills, weather, rights -- and roughly how. 10 = the personal stake is explicit and concrete; 0 = no implication that the reader's own circumstances are touched.

[X21] Explanation or analysis marker
Score high when the headline signals explainer or analytical framing ('Why...', 'Explaining...', 'The rise and fall'). Score low for breaking-event headlines.

[X22] Curiosity gap
Score high when the headline withholds key information the reader must click to resolve ('this photo', 'wasn't about Manafort', 'something in room'). Score low when the headline states its own conclusion.

[X23] Personal welfare stakes
High when it signals direct effects on people’s health, safety, rights, or daily routines; low when consequences remain remote or institutional; it does not apply without individual consequences

[X24] Contains the word 'you' or 'your'
Score 0-10 for occurrences of the literal tokens 'you', 'your' or 'yours' in the headline. 10 = several occurrences or one in the opening two words; 0 = none. Count the tokens only; do not judge whether the story actually concerns the reader.

[X25] Headline word count
Score 0-10 for how long the headline is in words. 10 = very long (13+ words); 5 = around 8-9 words; 0 = 4 words or fewer. Judge extent only -- do not consider whether the length serves the story.

[X26] Scare-quoted scepticism marker
Single quoted word casting doubt ('deceptive', 'unidentified', 'no value'). Scores low-to-moderate; hedge weakens urgency

[X27] POLARIZATION
Scores high when the headline presents opposing camps or a divisive proposition; low when it describes consensus or a technical matter. It does not apply when disagreement is not explicitly stated

[X28] Mundane-relatable action
Score high when the headline centres on an everyday, ordinary activity framed as remarkable (cutting a carrot, losing a teddy bear). Score low when actions are institutional or geopolitical.

[X29] Reader-addressing question
Headline is phrased as a question to the reader ("What do we know?", "How will we know...?"). Scores high; does not apply to reported questions

OUTPUT. Emit exactly one JSON object and nothing else:

{"verdicts": [
  {"id": "<the id shown>", "label": "quality_relevant" or "incidental",
    "mixed": true or false, "confidence": "high"/"medium"/"low",
    "justification": "<one sentence>"},
  ... one entry per criterion, same ids ...
]}

